 Understanding the SPI Waveform

The waveform represents various SPI signals over time (in nanoseconds). The key signals shown are:
•	clk (Clock) → System clock toggling at regular intervals.
•	cs (Chip Select) → Goes LOW when communication is active.
•	miso (Master In, Slave Out) → Data received from the slave.
•	mosi (Master Out, Slave In) → Data sent from the master.
•	reset → Initializes the system before communication starts.
•	sck (SPI Clock) → Controls timing for data transfer.

🔍 Key Observations

1.	Clock (clk & sck) Behavior
   clk toggles continuously.
   sck changes on every rising edge of clk, synchronizing SPI data.

2.	Chip Select (cs) Activation
   Cs starts HIGH → No communication.
   When cs goes LOW → SPI master starts data transfer.

3.	MOSI & MISO Data Flow
   When mosi = HIGH (1), the master sends 1 to the slave.
   Miso mirrors mosi, demonstrating a simple loopback.

4.	Reset Signal Impact
   Reset is HIGH initially, keeping SPI inactive.
   Reset goes LOW → SPI operation begins.

Summary of Waveform Timing
- 0 - 10 ns: Reset is HIGH → System is inactive.
- 10 - 50 ns: Reset LOW → Chip Select goes LOW → Communication starts.
- 50 - 110 ns: MOSI transmits data → MISO mirrors it → Clock cycles synchronize.
